  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nRationale: The existing solution focuses on optimizing a binary step function represented by 400 samples. The current C5 value of 0.380863 suggests we need to achieve a low value below 0.3808. The binary nature and step-function representation might be overly constrained. To improve, we should use a continuous representation and factor-based decomposition. We will allow h(x) to be any value in [0,1] rather than just 0/1, enabling smaller, more granular adjustments and potentially capturing peaks/troughs more effectively. The approach will also break the function into factors (on/off periods) and optimize the transition points, leveraging the observation that minimal overlap occurs at transitions between on and off states. The method will use probabilistic sampling, random projection, and coarse-to-fine optimization across different resolutions to avoid local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h will be represented as:\n- Neutral: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2]\n- Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1 (\u2192 sum(h)*dx = 1)\nThe objective is to minimize:\n   C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nWe will use a thresholding approach: Decompose the optimized h into a factored form\n   h(x) = \u220f_{i=1}^m (t_i(x))\nwith constraints 0\u2264t_i\u22641. This implicitly creates steps at the boundaries of the factors.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n**Parent validation and initialization**\n   - Validate input parent (if available) and ensure the length is 400. If not available, construct a default with projected uniform distribution.\n   - Transform the parent vector h into a factored representation with evenly spaced factors (e.g., 5-15 factors).\n\n**Core optimization loop**\n   - Phase 1: Low-resolution sampling (n=100,200) \u2192 start with fewer samples for speed\n   - Factor projection: Convert dense vector to fine-grained factor breaks\n   - Stochastic projection: Use random projection preserving integral\n   - Three-factor structured optimization: Use triple of (a,b,c) for on/off phases (shaped triangularly)\n\n**Candidate generation**\n   - Define candidate generator f(t)=1-|scaling_factor*(t - center)| optionally bounded by max/max_low\n   - Ensure candidate sum remains near 1 via linear scaling compensation\n\n**Constraint handling**\n   - Enforce [0,1] interval via clipping and linear projection\n   - Balance the integral via line search between projections\n\n**Best candidate tracking**\n   - Use time-based stopping criteria: track elapsed time, adjust resolution if needed\n   - Query objective every 100ms, return best candidate if time budget exhausted\n\n**Feasibility check**\n   - Pre-check with projected sum and value bounds\n   - Final validation using exact constraints\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial phase: Use small n=100 for rapid iteration (20 iterations)\n- Medium phase: n=200 for continued optimization (10 iterations)\n- Final phase: n=400 for high-resolution polish (1 iteration)\n- Factor control: Limit between 8-20 factors for manageable optimization\n- Projection: Use Johnson-Lindenstrauss-inspired dimension reduction for faster evaluation\n- Multi-scale optimization: Generate \u2192 prune samples \u2192 optimize in three resolutions\n- Random perturbation: Add multiplicative noise to break local optima\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- n=100: ~0.1s, n=200: ~0.25s, n=400: ~0.9s (SLQSQuadratic sits)\n- Estimate key timepoints (monitor every 25% budget)\n- Time budget: 850s max runtime\n- Negative updates: Refresh factorization if cost overflows\n\n**Implementation sketch:**\n```python\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    t0 = time.time()\n    total = budget_s - 8\n\n    # Phase1: Small resolution (100 samples)\n    n = 100\n    h = initial_h_values if n==100 else np.full(n, 0.5)  # Project parent vector to this resolution\n    best_c, best_h = 0., np.copy(h)\n\n    # Tunable parameters\n    factors = 10  # initial factor count\n    max_iterations = 20  # per resolution phase\n    \n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        # Candidate factorization: evenly spaced factors\n        factor_centers = np.linspace(0, n, factors+1)[1:-1]\n        factor_amplitudes = np.ones(factors) * 0.5\n        \n        # Project to [0,1] space\n        h_projected = project_feasible(h)\n        \n        # Optimization (simplified)\n        new_h = optimize_factored_representation(h_projected, factor_centers, factor_amplitudes)\n        \n        # Cost evaluation\n        c5 = compute_c5(new_h, n)\n        if c5 < best_c:\n            best_c, best_h = c5, new_h\n\n    # Phase2: Medium resolution (200 samples)\n    n=200\n    h = interpolate_projection(best_h, n)\n    ... [similar loop] ...\n\n    # Phase3: Final\n    n=400\n    h = interpolate_projection(best_h, n)\n    ... [evaluate and return] ...\n\n```\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Verify h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h)*dx \u2248 1 with tolerance 1e-5\n- Re-evaluate C5 on final candidate using np.correlate()\n- Return (h_400, best_c, n=400)\n- Fallback: If time exhausted, return best candidate from all phases",
